{"@context":["https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/ns/content/v1.jsonld",{"@base":"https://litlfred.github.io/folio/"}],"@id":"papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/prop-explicit-alpha-k-h4","@type":["folio-assistant-core:Proposition","doco:Section"],"label":"prop:explicit-alpha-k-h4","kind":"proposition","title":"Explicit $\\alpha_3$ at $H_4(R, q)$ via Wedderburn-orthogonal class-function $r_3$","uses":["papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/prop-wedderburn-bratteli-recursion","papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/rem-y-lambda-sign-pattern"],"tags":["alpha-k","h4","wedderburn-orthogonal","class-function-basis","hoefsmit","structural-derivation","depth-3-stratum"],"leanRef":"qou:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.explicit_alpha_k_h4","text":"explicit-alpha-k-h4.md","leanSource":"folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/explicit-alpha-k-h4.lean","provenance":"authored","html":"<a id=\"prop:explicit-alpha-k-h4\"></a><p><em>For each <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\lambda \\,\\vdash\\, 4</code>, the Wedderburn-orthogonal representative <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">r_\\lambda = \\sum_C c_C \\cdot T_{w_C}</code> has coefficients <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">c_C(\\lambda) \\in \\mathbb{Q}(q)</code> defined as:</em></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\lambda</code></th>\n<th><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">c_e</code></th>\n<th><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">c_{(12)}</code></th>\n<th><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">c_{(12)(34)}</code></th>\n<th><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">c_{(123)}</code></th>\n<th><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">c_{(1234)}</code></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">(4)</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\dfrac{1}{q^3 \\Phi_{(4)}}</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\dfrac{q^4 + 2q^2 + 3}{q^2 \\Phi_{(4)}}</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\dfrac{1}{q(q^2+1)}</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\dfrac{q^4 + q^2 + 2}{q (q^4 + q^2 + 1)}</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">1</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">(3,1)</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">-\\dfrac{1}{q(q^2+1)}</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\dfrac{1 - 2q^2 - q^4}{q^2 (q^2+1)}</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\dfrac{1}{q(q^2+1)}</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\dfrac{1 - q^2}{q}</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">1</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">(2,2)</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">-\\dfrac{q^2}{(q^2+1)^2}</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\dfrac{q(1-q^2)}{(q^2+1)^2}</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">-\\dfrac{q^4 + q^2 + 1}{(q^2+1)^2}</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">1</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">0</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">(2,1,1)</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\dfrac{q^3}{q^2+1}</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\dfrac{q^4 - 2q^2 - 1}{q^2+1}</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">-\\dfrac{q^3}{q^2+1}</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\dfrac{1 - q^2}{q}</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">1</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">(1^4)</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">-\\dfrac{q^9}{\\Phi_{(4)}}</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\dfrac{q^4(3q^4 + 2q^2 + 1)}{\\Phi_{(4)}}</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">-\\dfrac{q^3}{q^2+1}</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\dfrac{q(-2q^4 - q^2 - 1)}{q^4 + q^2 + 1}</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">1</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><em>where <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\Phi_{(4)} = q^6 + 2q^4 + 2q^2 + 1</code>.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Archimedean specialisation.</strong> What follows sits on the archimedean side of the substrate wall. It invokes real-analytic constructs. Any generic categorical identities remain valid over an abstract coefficient ring.</p>\n","source":"folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/explicit-alpha-k-h4.md","heading":"Proposition","number":"13.31.4"}
